message.content:
`uri_normalize` calls `uri_decoder` with `just_normalize = TRUE`. In `uri_decoder`, when `just_normalize` is TRUE and `*s == '%'`:

```c
if (!g_ascii_isxdigit (s[1]) ||
    !g_ascii_isxdigit (s[2]))
{
    /* % followed by non-hex; this is an error */
    if (flags & G_URI_FLAGS_PARSE_STRICT)
    {
        g_set_error_literal (error, G_URI_ERROR, parse_error,
                             _("Invalid %-encoding in URI"));
        g_free (decoded);
        return -1;
    }

    /* In non-strict mode, just let it through */
    *d++ = *s;
    continue;
}

c = HEXCHAR (s);
if (just_normalize && !g_uri_char_is_unreserved (c))
{
    /* Leave the % sequence there. */
    *d++ = *s;
}
else
{
    *d++ = c;
    s += 2;
}
```

When `just_normalize` is TRUE and the `%` is followed by two hex digits, `HEXCHAR(s)` is called which reads `s[1]` and `s[2]`. Then `g_uri_char_is_unreserved(c)` is checked. If `c` is unreserved, the `%` sequence is decoded. If `c` is not unreserved, the `%` sequence is left as-is.

But in all cases, `s[1]` and `s[2]` are read. If `s` is at position `end - 2` (second-to-last byte) and `*s == '%'`:
- `s[1]` = `end[-1]` = last byte of input
- `s[2]` = `end[0]` = null terminator (within buffer)

If `s[1]` is a hex digit and `s[2]` is '\0', `g_ascii_isxdigit('\0')` returns FALSE, so the condition is TRUE. The code copies `%` and continues. No OOB read.

If `s` is at position `end - 1` (last byte) and `*s == '%'`:
- `s[1]` = `end[0]` = null terminator (within buffer)
- `g_ascii_isxdigit('\0')` returns FALSE, so the condition is TRUE. The code copies `%` and continues. No OOB read.

So with null-terminated input, there's no OOB read in `uri_decoder`.

